= Propositional logic =

== Overview ==
'''Propositional logic''' studies formulas built from variables representing whole propositions or sentences and operators that combine them.

Classical propositional logic is one of the simplest major formal logical systems.

== Atomic propositions ==
Symbols such as:

<pre>
P, Q, R
</pre>

stand for whole propositions.

The system does not analyze their internal subject–predicate structure.

== Connectives ==
Common classical connectives include:

* negation: ¬P;
* conjunction: P ∧ Q;
* disjunction: P ∨ Q;
* conditional: P → Q;
* biconditional: P ↔ Q.

Complex formulas are built recursively from simpler formulas.

== Truth-functional semantics ==
In classical propositional logic, the truth value of a compound formula is determined by the truth values of its components.

Truth tables define the connectives and can test tautologies, contradictions, equivalence, and validity.

== Deduction ==
Proof systems such as natural deduction or tableaux derive conclusions using formal rules.

Soundness and completeness connect derivability to truth-table/model semantics.

== Limits ==
Propositional logic cannot directly express:

* “all objects have property P”;
* “some object has property P”;
* relations between objects;
* quantifier scope.

[[First-order logic]] adds these resources.

